@BadApi_HostAddress = http://localhost:5062

### DDoS lab: how to use this file
// This scenario is about denial-of-service behavior, not a normal CRUD endpoint.
// The goal is to observe how a slow, blocking request can tie up server resources.
//
// 1. Start the API using the normal project run command.
// 2. Keep the terminal or Aspire dashboard open so you can watch the API logs.
// 3. Send the requests below one after another, ideally in quick succession.
// 4. Compare the behavior of /ddos/bad, /ddos/better, and /ddos/good.
// 5. Then open BadApi/DDoS/Endpoints.cs and match the observed behavior to the code.
// 6. Finally, run the manual load-test app from this repo to simulate a real traffic spike.
//
// The important lesson is that a request can look harmless while still consuming
// server resources in a way that makes the app unresponsive.

### Manual load generator setup
// The separate DDoS app is not integrated with Aspire yet, so it must be started manually.
// From the repo root, run one of these examples:
//
//   dotnet run --project DDoS/DDoS.csproj -- bad
//   dotnet run --project DDoS/DDoS.csproj -- better
//   dotnet run --project DDoS/DDoS.csproj -- good
//
// This starts NBomber against the selected API endpoint and sends a high rate of requests to
// produce the resource exhaustion effect. The scenarion takes 5 minutes to complete.
//
// Watch the terminal while the load generator runs. You should see the server slow down,
// queue requests, and eventually become less responsive as the app struggles under load.
//
// Once the test completes, you can review the results int the HTML report. Look in the 
// reports folder. Pay special attention to the Throughput and Latency charts.

### 1. Vulnerable endpoint: blocking thread
// This endpoint intentionally sleeps for 5 seconds on the server thread.
// If you fire several requests quickly, the app will start to feel stuck.
POST {{BadApi_HostAddress}}/ddos/bad?id=demo-1
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### 2. Still bad: rate limiting does not fix a blocking server call
// This endpoint is still slow, even though a rate limit is applied.
// The point is to show that mitigation must target the actual resource issue.
POST {{BadApi_HostAddress}}/ddos/better?id=demo-2
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### 3. Better endpoint: asynchronous delay instead of blocking the thread
// This version uses await Task.Delay(...), so it does not block a request thread.
// The app can still process other work while this request waits.
POST {{BadApi_HostAddress}}/ddos/good?id=demo-3
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### Optional: repeat the requests several times
// Try sending a few requests in a row to each route.
// Compare the response times and whether the app stays responsive.

POST {{BadApi_HostAddress}}/ddos/bad?id=demo-4
Content-Type: application/json

{}

POST {{BadApi_HostAddress}}/ddos/good?id=demo-5
Content-Type: application/json

{}


